<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.3.4: Solving More Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00412</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.3.4: Solving More Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8adb98a6-0104-4325-acc4-1d59c772da57</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <para>Solve this system of equations without graphing. Be prepared to show your reasoning.
  Use this system of equations to answer questions 1 – 3.
</para>
      <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}5x\;+\;y\;=\;7\\y\;=\;-20x\;+\;2\end{array}\right.\)</para>
      <!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!--Q1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the \(x\) value?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> \(\frac15\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_1d10d402-b443-4531-bfcf-dc65abd33808" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the \(y\) value?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 6</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 6.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!-- END ACTIVITY INPUT 1 TRY -->
      <!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!--Q3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain your solutions.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
          <list>
            <item>Substituting \( 20x + 2 \) for \( y \) in the first equation, \( 5x+y=7 \), gives \( 5x + (20x+2) = 7 \) or \( 25x + 2 = 7 \), which then gives \( x=\frac15 \). Substituting \( \frac15 \) for \( x \) in the first equation and solving for \( y \) gives \( y=6 \).</item>
            <item>The first equation, \( 5x+y=7 \), can be rearranged to \( y = 7 - 5x \). Substituting \( 7-5x \) for \( y \) in the second equation gives \( 20x + 2 = 7-5x \). Solving this equation leads to \( x=\frac15 \), and substituting \( \frac15 \) for \( x \) in either equation gives \( y=6 \).</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <note>
        <para>Building Character: Growth Mindset</para>
        <div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-gap-1">
          <figure>
            <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_05_09_01.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
          <div>
            <para>Having a growth mindset means believing you can improve your abilities. Compared to a fixed mindset, a growth mindset encourages you to embrace challenges, sustain effort, and try new strategies.</para>
            <para>Think about your current sense of having a growth mindset. Are the following statements are true for you?</para>
            <list>
              <item>
          After I make a mistake, I always look for ways to learn from it.
       </item>
              <item>
          I love challenges because they make me smarter.
       </item>
            </list>
            <para>Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
          </div>
        </div>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
